        {
          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"target_identification\":{\"speaker\":\"Goneril\",\"addressee\":\"Lear\",\"anchor_quote\":\"Sir, I love you more than word can wield the matter;\\nDearer than eyesight, space, and liberty;\\nBeyond what can be valu’d, rich or rare;\\nNo less than life, with grace, health, beauty, honour;\\nAs much as child e’er lov’d, or father found;\\nA love that makes breath poor and speech unable;\\nBeyond all manner of so much I love you.\"},\"target_translation\":\"I love you.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"The focal words directly avow Goneril’s love for Lear. The remainder of her turn intensifies that asserted emotional state by presenting it as beyond expression and more valuable than life, liberty, and other goods.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love you more than word can wield the matter;\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Beyond all manner of so much I love you.\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.88,\"reason\":\"The expression asserts the magnitude of Goneril’s filial love but does not itself create, renew, or invoke a commitment or obligation. Its comparisons concern how much she loves Lear, not conduct to which she binds herself. Lear’s subsequent grant is a reward in the love contest, not uptake of a promise made by these words.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"As much as child e’er lov’d, or father found;\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"That we our largest bounty may extend\\nWhere nature doth with merit challenge.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.94,\"reason\":\"This is an extended, rhetorically structured answer to Lear’s formal demand, not a reflex-like affective discharge. Its hyperbole and intensity amplify an avowal but do not make it an involuntary or interjectional response.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Goneril,\\nOur eldest born, speak first.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"A love that makes breath poor and speech unable;\\nBeyond all manner of so much I love you.\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":2,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.83,\"reason\":\"Beyond avowing love, the occurrence functions as a public encomium and competitive claim that Goneril’s filial love is exceptionally great. That self-ranking, delivered in response to a contest tied to Lear’s bounty, is not fully captured by T/P/E.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Which of you shall we say doth love us most?\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love you more than word can wield the matter;\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Dearer than eyesight, space, and liberty;\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"In a public dynastic proceeding, Lear asks his daughters to declare which loves him most and explicitly links their answers to the distribution of his bounty. Goneril, called on first, answers with an elaborate claim of supreme filial love.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"unknown\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"No undertaking by Goneril is established in the focal expression or its turn: she claims extraordinary love without binding herself to specified or implied conduct. Lear immediately awards her territory, but the supplied scene represents this as the consequence of her successful answer to his love test, not acceptance of a commitment. The evidence does not settle whether her avowed affection is sincere.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"This passage is recognised as the opening love-test scene from William Shakespeare’s King Lear.\",\"limitations\":\"The supplied passage supports analysis of the utterance’s public, competitive force but does not independently establish Goneril’s sincerity, private intentions, or later fulfilment of any filial expectations.\"}"
        }
